WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.813
To close, here's what to
take back to the tenant.

2
00:00:02.813 --> 00:00:06.704
The library: build the prerequisites
before the first policy,

3
00:00:06.704 --> 00:00:09.603
measure readiness rather
than estimating it,

4
00:00:09.603 --> 00:00:13.724
create every policy in report-only,
and enforce the waves in order.

5
00:00:13.724 --> 00:00:18.608
Exceptions: every exclusion is a governed
group with an owner and an expiry,

6
00:00:18.608 --> 00:00:23.963
and the break-glass accounts are alerted
on every use and tested every ninety days.

7
00:00:23.963 --> 00:00:28.069
PIM: emergency accounts first,
then the c1 policy switched On,

8
00:00:28.069 --> 00:00:33.162
then named approver groups, with every
human administrator eligible only.

9
00:00:33.162 --> 00:00:35.877
Governance: packages that always expire,

10
00:00:35.877 --> 00:00:40.426
reviews of groups rather than
applications, and auto-apply turned on.

11
00:00:40.426 --> 00:00:44.032
Code: one writer per object
class, two people per change,

12
00:00:44.032 --> 00:00:46.837
and drift monitored against the baseline.

13
00:00:46.837 --> 00:00:51.405
And evidence: exported before
Microsoft's thirty-day window closes,

14
00:00:51.405 --> 00:00:53.199
and assembled every month.

15
00:00:53.199 --> 00:00:57.500
In Zero Trust, every access
decision is a policy decision.

16
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.958
This session has been about
making those policies explicit,

17
00:01:00.958 --> 00:01:04.900
testing them before they enforce,
and keeping the proof that they ran.

18
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.843
Keep Appendix A at hand for every policy's
template, Appendix B for every role's

19
00:01:09.843 --> 00:01:14.863
settings, and Book 05 for the device
compliance that Conditional Access reads.

20
00:01:14.863 --> 00:01:15.700
Thank you.
